questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where a distributed order service fails to persist audit logs when an external payment gateway call throws a `PaymentTimeoutException`. The developer implemented the following Spring `@Service` bean to ensure audit logs are written in a separate, isolated transaction regardless of main transaction failure:

java
@Service
public class OrderProcessingService {

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;
    @Autowired
    private AuditRepository auditRepository;

    @Transactional
    public void processOrder(OrderRequest request) {
        Order order = orderRepository.save(new Order(request));
        try {
            callPaymentGateway(request);
        } catch (PaymentTimeoutException ex) {
            this.logAuditFailure(order.getId(), ex.getMessage());
            throw ex;
        }
    }

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void logAuditFailure(Long orderId, String error) {
        auditRepository.save(new AuditLog(orderId, error));
    }
}


Despite `logAuditFailure` being annotated with `Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW`, the `AuditLog` entry is never saved to the database when `PaymentTimeoutException` is rethrown.
